# /etc/fw_env.config — where fw_printenv/fw_setenv (libubootenv-tool) find the
# redundant U-Boot environment pair.
#
# RAUC integration owns this file. It is the single fw_env.config source
# in the tree; there is deliberately no os/pkgs/rauc/fw_env.config.in to drift from
# it. It is rendered from os/boards/cx3576/board.env by os/rootfs/build.sh, and
# os/pkgs/rauc/render-config.sh asserts its contract (run on every bundle build):
# exactly two device lines, each addressing its UENV partition at offset 0 with
# size UENV_SIZE_BYTES, and those partition starts cross-checked against the
# byte offsets U-Boot's ENV_OFFSET/ENV_OFFSET_REDUND are pinned to.
#
# Two device lines is what marks the environment redundant to libubootenv. If
# only one side is configured redundant, every read from the other fails its
# CRC check.
#
# The pair is addressed by partition GUID rather than by a hardcoded
# /dev/mmcblk0 offset: the GUIDs are layout constants, the disk name is not.
#
# The environment addressed here exists on a mos device — the image carries the
# uboot-mos variant, which has the redundant pair at UENV_A/B_OFFSET_BYTES, and
# os/build/src/mkimage-cx3576.ts refuses to assemble a mos image around the debug variant.
#
# Two writers exist for this environment (RAUC slot marking and the first-boot
# machine-id oneshot), and the dashboard's boot-credit poll will add a reader
# with no boot-time ordering against either. The serialisation rule:
# every access goes through fw_printenv/fw_setenv. The shipped libubootenv
# (Debian trixie, 0.3.5) takes flock(LOCK_EX) on /var/lock/fw_printenv.lock
# inside libuboot_open and holds it across the whole open-read-modify-write,
# on the read path and the write path alike -- verified by strace of both
# tools on the pinned base image: the lock file is opened and flocked before
# either UENV copy is, so a reader cannot land mid-write. Raw access to the
# UENV partitions (dd, a private mmap) is forbidden: it bypasses the lock AND
# libubootenv's redundant-pair selection. Two caveats the rule stands on:
#
#   - libuboot_open IGNORES a lock failure (uboot_env.c:2059 in 0.3.5) and
#     proceeds UNLOCKED -- also verified: with /var/lock absent, fw_setenv
#     went ahead with no flock at all. /var/lock -> /run/lock is recreated
#     every boot by systemd-tmpfiles (legacy.conf) at sysinit, so every unit
#     touching this environment must keep DefaultDependencies=yes; the
#     implicit After=sysinit.target is what puts the lock directory in place.
#
#   - the flock covers one tool invocation, never a check-then-set spanning
#     two, so every variable has exactly one writer: machine_id belongs to
#     mos-machine-id, BOOT_ORDER and BOOT_A_LEFT/BOOT_B_LEFT belong to RAUC
#     (whose uboot backend execs these same tools). Readers may poll
#     anything; a second writer of an owned variable is a bug wherever it
#     appears.
#
# <device>                              <offset>  <env size>
/dev/disk/by-partuuid/@UENV_A_GUID@     0x0       @UENV_SIZE_HEX@
/dev/disk/by-partuuid/@UENV_B_GUID@     0x0       @UENV_SIZE_HEX@
